# config/Kconfig.arch — ISA / architecture selection
#
# RFC-0026 Phase C1.  Controls architecture-specific defaults downstream.
# Board choices (config/Kconfig.platform) must be consistent with the ISA selected.

menu "Architecture"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ISA choice
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

choice
    prompt "Instruction Set Architecture"
    default ARCH_RISCV64
    help
      Select the target ISA for this kernel build.  All downstream defaults
      (stack sizes, FP context, page sizes, WCET bounds) adjust automatically.
      Changing this choice requires a full `cargo clean` before rebuilding.

    config ARCH_RISCV64
        bool "RISC-V 64-bit (rv64imac / rv64gc)"
        help
          RISC-V 64-bit — the primary AZOS target.  Supports all five
          build profiles (edge/vf2, edge/k1, QEMU virt).
          ISA base: rv64imac minimum; optional extensions via Kconfig.

    config ARCH_AARCH64
        bool "Aarch64 (ARMv8.0-A and later)"
        help
          64-bit ARM — secondary target, demonstrated on cortex-a72 QEMU.
          Phase 2 multi-arch support.  Kernel builds but E2E robot loop
          is deferred to Phase 3 hardware bring-up.

    config ARCH_X86_64
        bool "x86_64 (port skeleton: type-checks only)"
        help
          The x86_64 port skeleton: every arch-api trait implemented with
          todo!() bodies (crates/core/arch-x86_64), the entry/asm/linker
          stubs under kernel/src/entry/x86_64/, and this Kconfig block. It
          type-checks (`make ARCH=x86_64 check`); it does not link or boot.
          Its purpose is to show, as code, what a new ISA must provide.

endchoice

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hardware support model (every ISA the same way)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. A BASELINE level per ISA (RISCV64_LEVEL, AARCH64_LEVEL, X86_64_LEVEL):
#    the only thing codegen assumes for the whole kernel and its user
#    images (tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py --rustflags / --user-rustflags), and
#    checked at boot: a CPU below it is refused with a message.
# 2. Every OPTIONAL extension is a three-way choice:
#      n        never used, even when the CPU has it (its path is compiled
#               out or never selected, and the vDSO hwcap hides it);
#      probe    compiled in, used only when the boot probe finds it, with
#               a fallback otherwise;
#      require  part of the baseline: codegen may assume it where the ISA
#               allows, and the boot refuses a CPU without it.
#    The boot prints one `[ISA]` line: the baseline and each extension's
#    state (n / probed-present / probed-absent / required).
# 3. Per-board defaults (`default ... if BOARD_*`) say what each board has.
# The policy values reach Rust as azos_arch_api::isa (crates/core/arch-api).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RISC-V specific options
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

if ARCH_RISCV64

choice
    prompt "riscv64 baseline ISA"
    default RISCV64_LEVEL_RV64GCV if BOARD_K1
    default RISCV64_LEVEL_RV64GC if BOARD_VF2
    default RISCV64_LEVEL_RV64IMAC
    help
      What the kernel and its user images assume of every hart. The
      kernel is soft-float (riscv64imac-unknown-none-elf, lazy FP), so the
      level changes codegen only through the bit-manipulation extensions
      set to `require` below; F, D and V are reached through
      `.option arch` inside their own save/restore and SIMD code. The boot
      checks cpu@0's device-tree `riscv,isa` against the level and refuses
      a hart below it. Raise it when every board the image targets has
      the extensions: nothing is gained in speed, but a wrong board is
      refused with a message instead of faulting later.

    config RISCV64_LEVEL_RV64IMAC
        bool "rv64imac (QEMU virt, any RV64 with atomics and C)"
        help
          Integer, multiply, atomics (Zaamo + Zalrsc) and compressed. The
          default; what every build assumed before this choice existed.
    config RISCV64_LEVEL_RV64GC
        bool "rv64gc (+ F, D, Zicsr, Zifencei: JH7110 / VisionFive 2)"
        help
          Adds the F and D floating-point registers the FP context
          (FP_HARDFLOAT_*) saves. The SiFive U74 of the VF2.
    config RISCV64_LEVEL_RV64GCV
        bool "rv64gcv (+ V 1.0: SpacemiT K1 / X60)"
        help
          Adds the vector extension: RV_V defaults to `require`. RVA22-class
          parts such as the X60 also mandate Zba/Zbb/Zbs (set below).
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Zicboz (cbo.zero page zeroing)"
    default RV_ZICBOZ_PROBE
    help
      `cbo.zero` zeroes a whole cache block per instruction in the page
      allocator's zero-fill (crates/core/arch-riscv64/src/cbo.rs): about a
      third of the cycles of a store loop per 4 KiB page under QEMU. probe:
      used when cpu@0's device tree declares it AND a trap-safe execution
      probe confirms it zeroes; plain stores otherwise. n: always plain stores.
      require: refuse to boot without it. Costs nothing when absent.

    config RV_ZICBOZ_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config RV_ZICBOZ_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config RV_ZICBOZ_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Sstc (S-mode stimecmp timer)"
    default RV_SSTC_PROBE
    help
      The timer is programmed by writing `stimecmp` (one CSR write) instead of
      an SBI `set_timer` call into firmware (an M-mode round trip, hundreds of
      cycles) on every tick and deadline (crates/drivers/irqchip/src/clint.rs).
      probe: used when cpu@0 declares Sstc and S-mode can read the CSR; SBI
      otherwise. n: always SBI. require: refuse to boot without it.

    config RV_SSTC_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config RV_SSTC_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config RV_SSTC_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Svpbmt (page-based memory types)"
    default RV_SVPBMT_PROBE
    help
      PTE bits that mark a page non-cacheable or I/O without PMAs. No kernel
      path uses it yet: n and probe differ only in the [ISA] boot line; require
      refuses a hart whose device tree does not declare it.

    config RV_SVPBMT_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config RV_SVPBMT_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config RV_SVPBMT_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Zba (address-generation bit-manipulation)"
    default RV_ZBA_REQUIRE if BOARD_K1
    default RV_ZBA_PROBE
    help
      sh1add/sh2add/sh3add and the .uw forms: shorter array indexing. No
      runtime-selected path uses it, so probe only reports it. require: the
      whole kernel and its user images are compiled with +zba (smaller, faster
      address arithmetic everywhere) and a hart without it is refused. The K1
      (RVA22) has it; the VF2's U74 build keeps it off.

    config RV_ZBA_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config RV_ZBA_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config RV_ZBA_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Zbb (basic bit-manipulation)"
    default RV_ZBB_REQUIRE if BOARD_K1
    default RV_ZBB_PROBE
    help
      Rotates, clz/ctz/cpop, rev8, andn/orn. probe: the kernel's SHA-256 (module
      and image digests) takes its rotate path when cpu@0 declares Zbb, and the
      vDSO hwcap advertises it to ring 3. n: the generic SHA-256 and no hwcap
      bit. require: compiled in everywhere with +zbb (bitmaps, hashing, byte
      swaps) and a hart without it is refused.

    config RV_ZBB_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config RV_ZBB_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config RV_ZBB_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Zbs (single-bit instructions)"
    default RV_ZBS_REQUIRE if BOARD_K1
    default RV_ZBS_PROBE
    help
      bset/bclr/binv/bext: one instruction per bit operation (ready bitmaps,
      PTE flags). No runtime-selected path, so probe only reports it.
      require: compiled in everywhere with +zbs; a hart without it is refused.

    config RV_ZBS_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config RV_ZBS_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config RV_ZBS_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "V 1.0 (vector)"
    default RV_V_REQUIRE if RISCV64_LEVEL_RV64GCV
    default RV_V_NEVER
    help
      The vector unit for the ML dot products and matmul (crates/core/ml,
      crates/drivers/camera) and the vector state saved on every context
      switch (`rvv` cargo feature, HAS_RVV). Cost when compiled in: the
      vector-state save/restore calls on every switch and up to 32 x VLEN/8
      bytes of state per task. probe: compiled in; the vector state and the
      vector kernels are used only when cpu@0 declares V, the scalar ones
      otherwise. n (the default without V hardware): compiled out, scalar
      kernels. require: a hart without V is refused.

    config RV_V_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config RV_V_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config RV_V_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "AIA (APLIC + IMSIC interrupt controller)"
    default RV_AIA_PROBE
    help
      Message-signalled interrupts through the IMSIC and the APLIC instead of
      the PLIC (crates/drivers/irqchip). probe: used when the device tree has
      an S-domain APLIC; the PLIC otherwise. n: the PLIC always, even on a
      machine with AIA (a machine with AIA and no PLIC then has no external
      interrupts). require: refuse to boot without an S-domain APLIC.

    config RV_AIA_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config RV_AIA_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config RV_AIA_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

config HAS_RVV
    bool
    default y if RV_V_PROBE || RV_V_REQUIRE
    help
      Derived from RV_V (n: off; probe or require: on). Compiles the
      vector code in (`rvv` cargo feature, FP_RVV).

config NO_MMU
    bool "No MMU mode (M+U only, no Sv39)"
    default n
    help
      Disables virtual-memory support and builds a flat-address kernel.
      For targets with no MMU hardware.  Incompatible with
      PROFILE_EDGE and PROFILE_FLEET.

config LOCKED_HUGE_LEAVES
    bool "Explicit 2 MiB leaves for big fixed regions of locked rows"
    depends on !NO_MMU
    default n
    help
      A ring-3 topology row with `mem = "locked"` may declare
      `mem_huge_mib = N` (N even, 2..512): N MiB of RAM reserved at boot,
      2 MiB-aligned and physically contiguous, and mapped into the task at
      exec with level-1 (2 MiB) Sv39 leaves instead of 512 page leaves each.
      One TLB entry then covers 2 MiB of a model, tensor arena or pipeline
      buffer (the U74's L1 DTLB has 40 entries; its L2 TLB holds 4 KiB
      entries only).

      The region is fixed for the task's life: never forked (a locked row
      cannot fork), never demand-paged, never COW, never returned to the
      allocator; munmap of any part of it is refused, and it is data only
      (read-write, not executable). It sits at the bottom of the task's
      shm/MMIO window (crates/core/sched/src/process.rs,
      LOCKED_ARENA_VA) and is zeroed at every exec. Memory admission
      counts it as locked pages (N x 256 topology pages). Only exec
      (autorun, SYS_EXEC) installs it; spawning such a row is refused.

      With this off, a topology that declares `mem_huge_mib` is refused at
      boot rather than run without the region. QEMU shows the mapping
      (page-table walk); it cannot show the TLB effect, which needs the
      board's DTLB counters.

endif # ARCH_RISCV64

if ARCH_AARCH64

choice
    prompt "aarch64 baseline architecture level"
    default AARCH64_LEVEL_8_2 if BOARD_RPI5
    default AARCH64_LEVEL_8_0
    help
      The Armv8.x level the kernel and its user images assume. Codegen
      gets that level's mandatory features (tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py:
      the kernel, soft-float, only those that need no FP/SIMD registers,
      e.g. +lse,+rcpc; the user images the whole list). The boot reads
      the ID registers before anything else and refuses a CPU below the
      level, naming the missing feature. A higher level makes atomics
      single instructions (8.1 LSE: a contended lock costs one LDADD
      instead of an LL/SC loop) and drops older cores.

    config AARCH64_LEVEL_8_0
        bool "Armv8.0 (Cortex-A53/A72, QEMU virt; the default)"
    config AARCH64_LEVEL_8_1
        bool "Armv8.1 (+ LSE atomics, CRC32, RDM, PAN, VHE)"
    config AARCH64_LEVEL_8_2
        bool "Armv8.2 (+ PAN2, DC CVAP, UAO: Cortex-A55/A76, Raspberry Pi 5)"
    config AARCH64_LEVEL_8_3
        bool "Armv8.3 (+ PAuth, RCpc, JSCVT, FCMA)"
    config AARCH64_LEVEL_8_4
        bool "Armv8.4 (+ DIT, FlagM, RCpc2, DotProd)"
    config AARCH64_LEVEL_8_5
        bool "Armv8.5 (+ BTI, SB, FlagM2, FRINTTS)"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "LSE atomics (FEAT_LSE)"
    default A64_LSE_REQUIRE if !AARCH64_LEVEL_8_0
    default A64_LSE_PROBE
    help
      LDADD/CAS/SWP: a single-instruction atomic instead of an LL/SC loop.
      The compiler can only emit them globally (no outlined atomics in this
      kernel), so probe detects and advertises them (vDSO hwcap) and require
      compiles the kernel and user images with +lse; Armv8.1 and above
      include it (a level >= 8.1 with LSE not `require` is a build error).

    config A64_LSE_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config A64_LSE_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config A64_LSE_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "CRC32 instructions (FEAT_CRC32)"
    default A64_CRC32_REQUIRE if !AARCH64_LEVEL_8_0
    default A64_CRC32_PROBE
    help
      CRC32B/H/W/X and CRC32C*. probe: advertised in the vDSO hwcap (ring-3
      and lxbase.ko CRC paths key on it). n: hidden. require: compiled in with
      +crc. Mandatory from Armv8.1.

    config A64_CRC32_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config A64_CRC32_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config A64_CRC32_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Pointer authentication (FEAT_PAuth)"
    default A64_PAUTH_REQUIRE if AARCH64_LEVEL_8_3 || AARCH64_LEVEL_8_4 || AARCH64_LEVEL_8_5
    default A64_PAUTH_NEVER if BOARD_RPI5
    default A64_PAUTH_PROBE
    help
      Signed return addresses. Detected and reported only: the kernel is not
      built with branch protection yet, so n and probe differ in the [ISA]
      line, and require refuses a CPU without it. Armv8.3 includes it. The
      Raspberry Pi 5's Cortex-A76 does not have it (default n there).

    config A64_PAUTH_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config A64_PAUTH_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config A64_PAUTH_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Branch target identification (FEAT_BTI)"
    default A64_BTI_REQUIRE if AARCH64_LEVEL_8_5
    default A64_BTI_NEVER if BOARD_RPI5
    default A64_BTI_PROBE
    help
      Indirect branches land only on BTI pads. Detected and reported only (no
      branch-protection build yet); require refuses a CPU without it. Armv8.5
      includes it; the Cortex-A76 does not.

    config A64_BTI_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config A64_BTI_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config A64_BTI_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Memory tagging (FEAT_MTE)"
    default A64_MTE_NEVER if BOARD_RPI5
    default A64_MTE_PROBE
    help
      4-bit tags per 16-byte granule, checked on every access. Optional at
      every level. Detected and reported only (no tagging allocator yet);
      require refuses a CPU without it. QEMU needs `-M virt,mte=on`.

    config A64_MTE_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config A64_MTE_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config A64_MTE_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "Scalable vectors (FEAT_SVE)"
    default A64_SVE_NEVER if BOARD_RPI5
    default A64_SVE_PROBE
    help
      Variable-length vectors. Detected and reported only: no kernel SVE
      path and no SVE state save (FP_SVE is not wired), so ring 3 must not
      use it; require refuses a CPU without it.

    config A64_SVE_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config A64_SVE_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config A64_SVE_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "AES instructions (FEAT_AES)"
    default A64_AES_PROBE
    help
      AESE/AESD/AESMC. probe: advertised in the vDSO hwcap; n hides it. They
      need the SIMD registers, so the soft-float kernel never compiles them
      globally; require adds +aes to the user images only and refuses a CPU
      without it.

    config A64_AES_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config A64_AES_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config A64_AES_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "64-bit polynomial multiply (FEAT_PMULL)"
    default A64_PMULL_PROBE
    help
      PMULL/PMULL2 on 64-bit lanes (GHASH, CRC folding). probe: vDSO hwcap;
      n hides it; require refuses a CPU without it.

    config A64_PMULL_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config A64_PMULL_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config A64_PMULL_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "SHA-256 instructions (FEAT_SHA256)"
    default A64_SHA2_PROBE
    help
      SHA256H/H2/SU0/SU1. probe: the kernel's SHA-256 (module verify, image
      digests) runs on them after its self-test, about 10x the generic
      blocks, and the vDSO hwcap advertises them. n: the generic SHA-256 and
      no hwcap bit. require: also +sha2 for the user images; a CPU without it
      is refused.

    config A64_SHA2_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config A64_SHA2_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config A64_SHA2_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

endif # ARCH_AARCH64

config MM_DEMAND_PAGING
    bool "Demand-paged anonymous mmap"
    depends on !NO_MMU
    default y
    help
      Wave 14 (DEMANDPAGE): an anonymous mmap (native SYS_MMAP and the
      Linux personality's) reserves its range and commits each page on
      the first touch, from the region's pager (zero-filled anonymous
      memory; crates/core/mm/src/pager.rs).  The reservation is charged
      to the task's page budget when it is made (RFC-0049 M1), so a
      fault never refuses on quota.  A kernel copy into a reserved page
      (read() into fresh mmap memory) commits it as the fault would.

      Pre-committed regardless: a mapping with MAP_POPULATE (0x8000) or
      MAP_LOCKED (0x2000), every mapping of a mem = "locked" task (which
      must take no page fault), and with MM_PRECOMMIT_RT every mapping
      of a real-time-class task.

      With this off every mmap commits every page at map time, the
      pre-wave-14 behaviour.  Cost: no static RAM beyond
      MM_REGIONS_PER_SPACE's table.

config MM_PRECOMMIT_RT
    bool "Pre-commit mmap for real-time-class tasks"
    depends on MM_DEMAND_PAGING
    default y
    help
      A task of class SafetyCritical, HardRT or SoftRT gets every mmap
      page committed at map time, as if it had passed MAP_POPULATE: a
      control loop never takes a demand fault, whose cost (a page zeroed
      in the fault path) would land inside its period.  Off: such tasks
      demand-fault like any other unless they ask for MAP_POPULATE.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# x86_64 (port skeleton)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The same three tiers riscv64 uses (crates/core/arch-riscv64/src/cbo.rs):
#   1. the compile-time BASELINE (X86_64_LEVEL), the only thing codegen may
#      assume, checked at boot: a CPU below it is refused with a message;
#   2. optional extensions, each a runtime CPUID probe before use with a
#      fallback, emitted only inside gated asm, never as a global
#      target-feature;
#   3. each extension a three-way choice X86_* (n / probe / require, the
#      model above); per-board defaults say what a board has.

if ARCH_X86_64

choice
    prompt "x86_64 baseline micro-architecture level"
    default X86_64_LEVEL_V2
    help
      The psABI level codegen assumes for the whole kernel
      (`-C target-cpu=x86-64[-vN]`, tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py). Anything
      above it is an optional extension (X86_* below). The boot CPU
      checks the level's CPUID bits before anything else and refuses to
      boot below it. Higher levels let the compiler use wider instructions
      everywhere (smaller, faster code) and drop every CPU older than the
      level.

    config X86_64_LEVEL_V1
        bool "x86-64 (v1: SSE2)"
        help
          Every x86_64 CPU. No popcnt/SSE4.2 in compiled code.
    config X86_64_LEVEL_V2
        bool "x86-64-v2 (SSE4.2, POPCNT, CMPXCHG16B)"
        help
          Nehalem (2008) and later, every QEMU `-cpu` model newer than
          qemu64 with `+sse4.2,+popcnt`. The default.
    config X86_64_LEVEL_V3
        bool "x86-64-v3 (AVX2, BMI2, FMA, MOVBE)"
        help
          Haswell (2013) and later. AVX2 becomes baseline; kernel FP/SIMD
          use still needs the FPU-section discipline (FP_XSAVE_EAGER).
endchoice

choice
    prompt "SMEP (supervisor-mode execution prevention)"
    default X86_SMEP_PROBE
    help
      CR4.SMEP after CPUID.(EAX=7,ECX=0):EBX[7]. Cost: none at runtime.
      Off: the kernel may execute user pages (an exploit primitive).
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_SMEP_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_SMEP_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_SMEP_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "SMAP (supervisor-mode access prevention)"
    default X86_SMAP_PROBE
    help
      CR4.SMAP after CPUID.(EAX=7,ECX=0):EBX[20]; the user-access window
      (ArchPlatform::user_access) is stac/clac. Cost: two instructions per
      user copy. Fallback: no window (every kernel access to user memory
      allowed), as without the bit.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_SMAP_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_SMAP_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_SMAP_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "PCID / INVPCID (tagged TLB)"
    default X86_PCID_PROBE
    help
      CR4.PCIDE after CPUID.1:ECX[17] (PCID) and CPUID.7:EBX[10] (INVPCID).
      Keeps TLB entries across address-space switches (the ASID of
      Mmu::switch_pt). Fallback: a full flush on every CR3 write.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_PCID_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_PCID_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_PCID_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "FSGSBASE (rd/wrgsbase)"
    default X86_FSGSBASE_PROBE
    help
      CR4.FSGSBASE after CPUID.7:EBX[0]: Cpu::percpu_base/set_percpu_base
      without an MSR access (tens of cycles less per kernel entry).
      Fallback: rdmsr/wrmsr IA32_GS_BASE.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_FSGSBASE_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_FSGSBASE_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_FSGSBASE_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "LAPIC TSC-deadline timer"
    default X86_TSC_DEADLINE_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.1:ECX[24]: Interrupts::set_timer_deadline writes
      IA32_TSC_DEADLINE in TSC ticks. Fallback: the LAPIC one-shot count
      calibrated against the TSC.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_TSC_DEADLINE_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_TSC_DEADLINE_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_TSC_DEADLINE_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "x2APIC (MSR-mapped LAPIC)"
    default X86_X2APIC_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.1:ECX[21]: LAPIC registers as MSRs, IPIs as one wrmsr to the
      ICR. Fallback: xAPIC MMIO at the IA32_APIC_BASE address.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_X2APIC_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_X2APIC_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_X2APIC_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "XSAVEOPT / XSAVES (compact, modified-only FP save)"
    default X86_XSAVEOPT_PROBE
    help
      CPUID.(EAX=0xD,ECX=1):EAX[0] (XSAVEOPT), [3] (XSAVES). Smaller and
      faster FP save on every switch. Fallback: XSAVE, then FXSAVE.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_XSAVEOPT_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_XSAVEOPT_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_XSAVEOPT_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "AVX2 in the Vector impl"
    default X86_AVX2_REQUIRE if X86_64_LEVEL_V3
    default X86_AVX2_NEVER
    help
      CPUID.7:EBX[5] plus XCR0 YMM state. Used ONLY inside the Vector
      impl's dot product, behind the probe and the FPU section. Fallback:
      SSE2 (baseline). Cost: the YMM half of the FP save area.
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_AVX2_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_AVX2_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_AVX2_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

choice
    prompt "SHA-NI (SHA-256 instructions)"
    default X86_SHA_NI_NEVER
    help
      CPUID.7:EBX[29]: the secure-boot hash through sha256rnds2. Fallback:
      the portable SHA-256 (crates/core/crypto).
      n: the fallback always. probe: the CPUID probe picks. require: the
      boot refuses a CPU without it.

    config X86_SHA_NI_NEVER
        bool "n: never use it"
    config X86_SHA_NI_PROBE
        bool "probe: use it when the boot finds it, fall back otherwise"
    config X86_SHA_NI_REQUIRE
        bool "require: baseline; refuse to boot without it"
endchoice

endif # ARCH_X86_64

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Floating-point context
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

choice
    prompt "Floating-point context save format"
    default FP_HARDFLOAT_D if ARCH_RISCV64
    default FP_NEON        if ARCH_AARCH64
    default FP_XSAVE_EAGER if ARCH_X86_64
    help
      Selects the FP register save format used in each task's TCB.
      Larger contexts cost more RAM per task; smaller contexts limit
      which FP instructions user code can use.

    config FP_SOFTFLOAT
        bool "No FP save area (soft-float)"
        help
          Minimal TCB; valid only if NO userspace code uses FP.
          Use for deeply embedded targets where FP is emulated in
          software and context save is unnecessary.

    config FP_HARDFLOAT_F
        bool "RISC-V F extension (32 × f32 = 128 B)"
        depends on ARCH_RISCV64
        help
          Saves only the 32 single-precision FP registers.  Sufficient
          if userspace never uses double-precision or vector ops.

    config FP_HARDFLOAT_D
        bool "RISC-V D extension (32 × f64 = 256 B, default rv64)"
        depends on ARCH_RISCV64
        help
          Saves all 32 double-precision FP registers.  The standard
          choice for rv64gc targets.  Required for drone attitude math.

    config FP_RVV
        bool "RISC-V V extension (32 × vlen-bit + control regs)"
        depends on ARCH_RISCV64 && HAS_RVV
        help
          Full RVV save area.  Context size depends on VLEN (K1: 256 bits
          = 1 KiB; QEMU default 128 bits = 512 B).  Use only when
          userspace RVV programs need preemptive multitasking.

    config FP_NEON
        bool "Aarch64 NEON (32 × 128-bit q-regs = 512 B)"
        depends on ARCH_AARCH64
        help
          Standard Aarch64 FP/SIMD save area.  Required for any user-mode
          code using NEON intrinsics or Rust's SIMD types.

    # FP_SVE's "&& HAS_SVE" dependency dropped 2026-09-25 along with HAS_SVE
    # itself (Q4.1). FP_SVE has zero Rust consumers of its own (grepped
    # alongside HAS_SVE) so this is an adjacent, not-yet-audited dead
    # option in its own right — left selectable on ARCH_AARCH64 alone
    # rather than deleted outright, since deleting it was not part of this
    # decision's scope. Worth a follow-up audit pass.
    config FP_SVE
        bool "Aarch64 SVE (variable-length, up to 2048-bit per reg)"
        depends on ARCH_AARCH64
        help
          Saves the full SVE register file.  Context size is
          VLEN-dependent and determined at runtime.  Adds allocation
          overhead to task creation.

    config FP_XSAVE_EAGER
        bool "x86_64 XSAVE area, EAGER switch (512 B legacy + XSAVE header + enabled components)"
        depends on ARCH_X86_64
        help
          x86_64 saves and restores the FP/SIMD state on EVERY context
          switch, never lazily: lazy switching (CR0.TS + #NM) is the LazyFP
          leak (CVE-2018-3665), and Linux removed it. aarch64's lazy path
          (kernel/src/entry/aarch64/fp_lazy.rs, CPACR_EL1 trap on first use)
          is a per-ISA choice, not a shared one. Cost: one XSAVEOPT/XRSTOR
          pair per switch (X86_XSAVEOPT), ~0.5-1 KiB per task.

endchoice

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MMU page size
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# riscv64: the base page is 4 KiB and is not a choice — Sv39 has no other
# base page. Larger translations on riscv64 are explicit level-1 (2 MiB)
# leaves for named regions, not a different base page (LOCKED_HUGE_LEAVES
# below).
#
# aarch64: VMSAv8-64 has three translation granules and the whole system
# (kernel and every user image) uses the one chosen here. It reaches the
# build twice and the kernel refuses to compile if the two disagree: as the
# cargo feature `page-16k` / `page-64k` (tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py emits it)
# that sets azos_arch_api::PAGE_SIZE, and as azos_limits::PAGE_SHIFT
# from this file. User images must be linked for the same granule
# (`make AARCH64_PAGE_SIZE=16384 ...`, see the Makefile).

choice
    prompt "Page size (aarch64 translation granule)"
    depends on ARCH_AARCH64
    default AARCH64_PAGE_4K
    help
      The base page of every mapping, kernel and user. A larger page holds
      more per TLB entry and needs fewer page-table levels' worth of tables
      for a dense region; it also rounds every per-task allocation (stack,
      ELF segment, page table, vDSO page) up to the larger size, so a small
      task costs more memory. Frame budgets (`mem_pages` in the topology)
      count pages of this size.

    config AARCH64_PAGE_4K
        bool "4 KiB (39-bit VA, 3 levels)"
        help
          TG0/TG1 = 4 KiB, T0SZ = T1SZ = 25. The default, and the only page
          size riscv64 has.

    config AARCH64_PAGE_16K
        bool "16 KiB (39-bit VA, 3 levels)"
        help
          TG0/TG1 = 16 KiB with the same 39-bit input range as the 4 KiB
          build, so every virtual address (user layout, vDSO, the kernel's
          upper half at 0xFFFF_FF80_0000_0000) is unchanged; the root table
          has 8 live entries. Level-1 blocks are 32 MiB. The CPU must
          implement the granule (ID_AA64MMFR0_EL1.TGran16): the kernel stops
          at boot with a message if it does not. QEMU `-cpu max` does.

    config AARCH64_PAGE_64K
        bool "64 KiB (48-bit VA, 3 levels)"
        help
          TG0/TG1 = 64 KiB. Three levels need a 48-bit input range at this
          granule (39 bits would walk two), so the kernel's upper half moves
          to 0xFFFF_0000_0000_0000. Level-1 blocks are 512 MiB. The CPU must
          implement the granule (ID_AA64MMFR0_EL1.TGran64).

endchoice

config PAGE_SHIFT
    int
    default 16 if AARCH64_PAGE_64K
    default 14 if AARCH64_PAGE_16K
    default 12
    help
      log2 of the base page size, derived from the choice above (12 on
      riscv64). Exported as azos_limits::PAGE_SHIFT.

endmenu # Architecture
